ml216x-printer-app (2.0.0~alpha-9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Works with two libpappl releases now: the 1.3.1 Debian ships in stable,
    testing and unstable alike, which is what this package depends on and is
    built against, and upstream PAPPL 1.4.12 (decision Q-27). Both are tested
    on every push. Nothing about installing this package changes.
  * Why 1.4.12 is worth having: it fixes four of the six libpappl defects
    docs/SECURITY-REVIEW.md records, including the two memory corruption bugs
    that any client able to open the IPP port can reach (S-1 and S-2). That was
    verified here rather than read off a changelog — the same two inputs kill a
    1.3.1 server and cannot make a 1.4.12 one fall over. Two defects are
    untouched by it, S-4 and S-5, and this release's own workarounds for them
    stay in place.
  * Debian packages no 1.4.x in any suite, so using it means building it:
    scripts/install-pappl-1.4.sh does that, checking the tarball against a
    recorded sha256 first, and README.md has the two environment variables that
    then select it. Only the machine running the server needs this; a computer
    printing to the queue speaks IPP and needs no libpappl at all. A .deb built
    this way still declares the archive's libpappl1t64, because that is the
    library it will meet on a user's machine.
  * Bug fix that only 1.4.x users would have met: every print job failed at its
    first page against 1.4, because the two releases number a job's pages
    differently — 1.3 calls the first page 1 and 1.4 calls it 0 — and this
    driver required its own count to match exactly. It now reads the numbering
    off each job. The bytes sent to the printer are unchanged, which all 32
    golden streams confirm.

 -- Çağan ERGÜN <caganergun@tutanota.com>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 14:20:00 +0300

ml216x-printer-app (2.0.0~alpha-8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Follow PAPPL's normal DNS-SD discovery and direct network IPP model.
    Serve on IPv4/IPv6 wildcard addresses at port 8631; CUPS sharing is optional.
    Remote administration remains disabled; file-output harnesses stay local.
  * Remove DNS-SD name clearing and the duplicated state-path/parser code.
    PAPPL's mainloop resumes state persistence at the existing path.
  * Update server regression checks and document discovery, optional permanent
    queues, upgrade restart, and the network exposure of dependency defects.

 -- Çağan ERGÜN <caganergun@tutanota.com>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0300

ml216x-printer-app (2.0.0~alpha-7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixes two crashes that 2.0.0~alpha-6 shipped. Both were reproduced here,
    and both are in this release's regression harness
    (scripts/server-probe.py), which was run against the alpha-5 and alpha-6
    binaries to confirm it catches them.
  * "ml216x-printer-app devices" no longer aborts. Reported by a user against
    alpha-6: the command died with "Unable to initialize DNS-SD: Daemon not
    running" followed by an assertion inside libavahi. Declining the DNS-SD
    announcement in alpha-6 worked by making D-Bus unreachable for the whole
    process, which also broke the code that *looks* for printers — libpappl
    passes the resulting null client to Avahi without checking it. The same
    listing runs inside the server for the web interface's "Add Printer" page,
    so opening that page killed the print server outright.
  * Every page of the web interface no longer kills the server. Opening
    http://localhost:8631/ — which README.md recommends — segfaulted the
    server on alpha-5 and alpha-6: libpappl looks up the footer HTML in its
    translation table on every page and looks up a null rather than skipping
    it. This release gives it a footer, so the lookup has something to find.
  * The server is still not discoverable, and now says so by saying nothing
    (decision Q-24). It gives its printers no DNS-SD name, which is the
    condition libpappl itself checks before advertising, so no announcement is
    attempted. The "Unable to initialize DNS-SD: Daemon not running" lines
    alpha-6 asked you to expect are gone; if you still see them, you are still
    running alpha-6.
  * Consequence of the above, stated because it changes where printers are
    kept from: the application now loads and saves its own state file, at the
    path libpappl's mainloop was already using
    (~/.config/ml216x-printer-app.state for a login session,
    /var/lib/ml216x-printer-app.state for a root server). Printers added with
    earlier versions are picked up unchanged; the upgrade needs nothing.
  * One gap, documented rather than hidden: a printer added through the web
    interface's own "Add Printer" form is advertised until the service is next
    restarted, because libpappl publishes it there itself. Adding printers
    with "ml216x-printer-app add", as README.md documents, is unaffected.
  * The three libpappl defects behind all of this are written up in
    docs/SECURITY-REVIEW.md as S-5, S-6 and S-7, with a second Debian bug
    report drafted in docs/DEBIAN-BUG-DRAFT.md. None of them is fixed here;
    what changes is that this application no longer walks into them.

 -- Çağan ERGÜN <caganergun@tutanota.com>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:20:00 +0300

ml216x-printer-app (2.0.0~alpha-6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The server no longer announces itself over DNS-SD (decision Q-23). It binds
    127.0.0.1 only, so the service it used to publish named a port nothing off
    the machine could open — and CUPS turns a discovered printer into a
    temporary queue named <printer>_<host>, which cannot be deleted for good
    because it is re-created from the announcement rather than stored. Users of
    2.0.0~alpha-5 and earlier who saw a second, undeletable queue were seeing
    this. Delete it once after upgrading and it stays gone.
  * Expected, and not a fault: the service log now carries "Unable to
    initialize DNS-SD: Daemon not running" twice at every start. That is how
    the announcement is declined — libpappl offers no supported way to say no,
    for reasons docs/DECISIONS.md Q-23 records in full — and the server serves
    IPP on the loopback address exactly as before.
  * Sharing to other machines is unaffected. That runs through CUPS on port
    631, which advertises its own queue; avahi-daemon therefore stays in
    Recommends. README.md now documents giving CUPS the queue explicitly with
    lpadmin -m everywhere, rather than waiting for a discovery that needs a
    browser Debian 13 no longer installs.
  * debian/copyright: the two FFI crates are MIT rather than Apache-2.0 OR MIT.
    Under an OR licence a consumer may always elect MIT, so the Apache arm
    guaranteed nothing while costing a second licence text; the GPL-2.0-only
    parts are unchanged and still forced by SpliX.
  * Source-tree only, with no effect on what this package installs: the frozen
    1.x CUPS filter was deleted at migration gate P11. Its page loop and its 61
    tests moved into the spl2-core crate, so the golden corpus that pins the
    printer's bytes outlived it; the 1.x binary remains buildable from the
    annotated tag v1.x-final.

 -- Çağan ERGÜN <caganergun@tutanota.com>  Thu, 10 Sep 2026 19:40:00 +0300

ml216x-printer-app (2.0.0~alpha-5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Renamed from samsung-ml2160-rust, following the repository rename. The
    binary, the unit, the udev rule and every path they use are unchanged; what
    moves is the package name, its documentation directory
    (/usr/share/doc/ml216x-printer-app) and the homepage.
  * Declares Conflicts/Replaces/Provides on samsung-ml2160-rust, since both
    install /usr/bin/ml216x-printer-app and cannot be co-installed. Installing
    this package therefore removes the old one, and that removal is what stops
    and disables the root system service of 2.0.0~alpha-3 and earlier. postinst
    keeps a backstop for the case where a dangling enablement symlink survives
    anyway: a root server holding port 8631 would keep the user service from
    ever binding.
  * The package description now points at the v1.x-final tag rather than the
    legacy/cups-filter-1.x branch, which has been deleted: the project supports
    the 2.0 line only, and the branch carried no commit the tag and main do not.
  * Note for anyone who installed the old package: apt handles the transition,
    but its per-user state is not touched by any of this. Printers you added
    live in ~/.config/ml216x-printer-app.state and stay there.

 -- Çağan ERGÜN <caganergun@tutanota.com>  Sun, 06 Sep 2026 23:30:00 +0300

samsung-ml2160-rust (2.0.0~alpha-4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The application now runs entirely in user space (decision Q-18). The
    package installs a systemd *user* unit at /usr/lib/systemd/user and
    enables it with `systemctl --global enable`; it no longer installs a
    system unit and no longer runs anything as root. PAPPL keeps its state in
    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ml216x-printer-app.state and its control socket in
    $TMPDIR, so `devices`, `add`, `status` and the rest work without sudo.
  * The spool moves from /var/spool/ml216x-printer-app to the service's
    RuntimeDirectory under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, created 0700. The binary now also
    sets 0700 on whatever --spool-directory it is given, so a manual run keeps
    the guarantee the old postinst's `chmod 700` provided: a raster job waiting
    to be converted is not readable by another local user.
  * The udev rule for the hardware-confirmed Samsung 04e8:330f device now also
    tags it `uaccess`, so the user of the active local session can open its
    /dev/bus/usb node. Debian's own rule leaves it root:lp 0664, which serves
    CUPS' root backend but not a printer application running as the user.
    Nothing changes for any other device.
  * The control socket moves out of /tmp (decision Q-19). libpappl creates it
    mode 0777, and the subcommands reached through it are the server's whole
    control surface, so in /tmp any local user could drive the server — as they
    could drive the root server this release replaces. The binary now points
    TMPDIR at $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR when the caller has set none and that directory
    is private, so the socket sits where only its owner can reach it. An
    explicit TMPDIR still wins, and a context with neither variable gets a
    warning rather than a silent exposure.
  * Upgrading from 2.0.0~alpha-3 or earlier stops and disables the old root
    service before enabling the user one, because the root server would
    otherwise keep port 8631 and the replacement would fail to bind. Purging
    still removes the root service's /var/lib state and /var/spool directory;
    it never touches per-user state in a home directory.

 -- Çağan ERGÜN <caganergun@tutanota.com>  Sun, 06 Sep 2026 22:45:00 +0300

